SEC 1.  THEOREM X1, the general cross-term reduction.
    X_{d,j} = sum_{r,r'} kappa^{(d,j)}_{r,r'} Theta_j[r,r']
    must equal sum_{|S|=|T|=j} per(z[S|T]) sigma_{d-j}(L^(S,T)).
    n=5: exact at every (d,j) with 2 <= d <= 5: True
    n=6: exact at every (d,j) with 2 <= d <= 6: True
    n=7: exact at every (d,j) with 2 <= d <= 5: True

SEC 2.  THEOREM X2, the survivor set and its count.
    n=5: survivor-restricted sum equals the full sum: True
    n=6: survivor-restricted sum equals the full sum: True
    |S(j,m)| closed form vs enumeration, j<=7, m<=6: True
    N(d) closed form, 3 <= d <= 11: True   values [3, 8, 17, 31, 51, 78]
    N(1) = 0, N(2) = 0  -- the d = 2 cross part is EMPTY, not merely small

SEC 3.  THE ATOM EXPANSION AND THE ANNIHILATION PRINCIPLE.
    Theta_j[r,r'] expands over pairs of set partitions.  A term dies
    exactly when some degree-1 vertex is undecorated, because a lone
    line sum of z appears.  Dropping those changes NOTHING.
    dropped expansion = Theta: True   undropped = Theta: True   j=1 rule: True

